<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elephanta Caves</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th–6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Elephanta Caves are rock-cut cave temples on Elephanta Island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a> harbour, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, mostly dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and carved in the 5th to 6th centuries. The main cave holds a huge three-headed image of Shiva, the Sadashiva. Their patrons are uncertain, and some historians attribute them to the Kalachuris of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahishmati">Mahishmati</a>. They became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1987.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elephanta-caves.jpg" alt="Elephanta Caves" width="320" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Haugen · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elephanta_Caves_Trimurti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
